'''Argas''' (är′gas), ''n.'' [NL., prob. < Gr. ἀργός, contr. of ἀεργός, not working, idle; cf. ἀργήεις, Doric ἀργᾶς, bright, shining.] A genus of mites, of the family ''Ixodidæ'', having no eyes. {{smaller|The best-known species is ''A. reflexus'', a parasite of birds, especially doves, and known as the ''dove-tick''. Other species are ''A. persicus'' and ''A. nigra''.}}

'''Argasidæ''' (är-gas′i-dē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Argas'' + ''-idæ''.] A family of ticks, named from the genus ''Argas''. Also ''Argantidæ''.

